#
# Catch-all backstop, so the next working doc is blocked the day it lands
# instead of the day someone remembers this file. Netlify matches top-down and
# the first match wins, so this goes AFTER the explicit lines: those keep firing
# for the docs we know about, and a future "this one .md IS meant to be
# served" exception can be added above this line and actually take effect.
#
# TODO(sofia): this line is UNVERIFIED and must not be trusted on its own.
# Netlify's splat matches the remainder of a path, and a trailing literal after
# the splat (the ".md" here) is not a documented pattern - it may match nothing
# at all. Every .md in the repo is now listed explicitly above, so the site is
# safe either way, but check this against a real deploy by requesting
# /CLAUDE.md with that explicit line temporarily removed. Until then, add an
# explicit line for every new working doc rather than relying on this.
/*.md  /404.html  404!

# build scripts live in the repo so the sitemap cannot drift, but they are not
# part of the site. Netlify has no build step here, so they ship with the upload.
/tools/*  /404.html  404!

# the build-pass specs and measurement scripts. Untracked and not gitignored,
# so a folder upload would serve them at /.work/spec-jsonld.md and friends.
/.work/*  /404.html  404!

# the Netlify subdomain serves the whole site a second time (verified 200 on
# every path). Every page canonicals to the apex, so Google consolidates — this
# just stops the duplicate being reachable at all.
https://sofiamiyakephoto.netlify.app/* https://sofiamiyakephoto.com/:splat 301!

